WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.984
tmpfs is a memory-backed filesystem commonly used for temporary storage on Linux. Files stored in tmpfs live in virtual memory.

2
00:00:08.984 --> 00:00:22.044
Depending on system configuration, pressure, and swap behavior, tmpfs pages may interact with swap, but the important design idea is that tmpfs is not ordinary persistent disk storage.

3
00:00:22.044 --> 00:00:29.863
It is useful for temporary files, runtime state, caches, and writable layers that do not need to survive reboot.

4
00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:38.930
Initramfs is different but related to memory-backed booting. It is an early boot environment loaded into memory with the kernel.

5
00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:45.501
The initramfs can contain tools and scripts needed before the final root filesystem is ready.

6
00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:56.232
In RAM-boot designs, initramfs logic may copy images, prepare overlays, load drivers, mount devices, or decide which filesystem becomes the real root.

7
00:00:56.232 --> 00:01:00.225
RAM-backed storage competes with the rest of the system.

8
00:01:00.225 --> 00:01:13.035
A root image copied into RAM, a tmpfs upper layer, browser memory, desktop graphics, containers, package updates, build tools, file cache, and applications all share finite memory.

9
00:01:13.035 --> 00:01:18.608
A system that boots successfully with no workload may fail under realistic use.

10
00:01:18.608 --> 00:01:27.010
A desktop with a browser, GPU stack, and development tools has different pressure than a kiosk running one application.

11
00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.671
Volatility is the defining tradeoff.

12
00:01:29.671 --> 00:01:37.990
If changes live only in RAM, they disappear when the system reboots, crashes, loses power, or discards the tmpfs layer.

13
00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.317
That can be excellent for clean labs and kiosks.

14
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:51.382
It can also lose logs, package updates, SSH host keys, machine identity, browser profiles, and user documents if persistence was not designed.

15
00:01:51.382 --> 00:02:00.532
Before committing to any design, measure. Watch memory use after boot, under real workload, during updates, and during shutdown.

16
00:02:00.532 --> 00:02:09.516
Consider whether swap is present and whether swap changes the privacy or performance expectations. Test low-memory behavior.

17
00:02:09.516 --> 00:02:15.755
Document what is volatile, what is persistent, and what happens when RAM pressure rises.

18
00:02:15.755 --> 00:02:21.328
A RAM-backed system is only reliable if its memory budget matches the workload.
